<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Slivnitsa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1885">1885</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbo-bulgarian-war">Serbo-Bulgarian War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Slivnitsa was fought from 17 to 19 November 1885 near the town of Slivnitsa, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a>, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbo-bulgarian-war">Serbo-Bulgarian War</a>. The Bulgarian army under Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-of-battenberg">Alexander of Battenberg</a> defeated the invading Serbian army of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> I. The victory secured the union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> with Eastern Rumelia, proclaimed in September 1885, which the powers accepted in 1886.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-slivnitsa.jpg" alt="Battle of Slivnitsa" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dimitar Gjudzenov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Slivnitsa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1877-1878">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1877–1878 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a> left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> divided: the lands north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-mountains">Balkan Mountains</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a> became an autonomous principality, while Eastern Rumelia, between the Balkan and Rhodope mountains, was semi-autonomous under an Ottoman-appointed Christian governor. Relations between the principality and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> cooled after 1883, and Russia came to oppose reunification.</p><p>When a rebellion broke out in Eastern Rumelia in September 1885, Prince Alexander Battenberg chose to lead the national movement rather than lose his throne. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> recalled all its officers, leaving the Bulgarian Army almost without commanders above the rank of captain. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> massed its forces in Eastern Rumelia against an Ottoman attack that never came; the threat came instead from King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>'s Serbia, which demanded compensation for the change in the Balkan balance of power.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> declared war on 13 November 1885 and crossed the lightly defended border in three columns, aiming to concentrate four divisions before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a>. Alexander moved his army west by every means available, one regiment marching 95 km in 32 hours, while light forces slowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> in the mountains. Reinforcements gathered at a prepared position at Slivnitsa, 30 km north-west of Sofia: nearly 4 km of trenches and redoubts on a ridge, commanded by Major Guchev.</p><p>On 17 November a Bulgarian advance on the right surprised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> division, and the main Serbian attack on the centre, poorly supported by artillery, was driven back with some 1,200 casualties. On the 18th two battalions of the Preslav Regiment arrived in time to hold the weaker left. On the 19th a Serbian flanking attack near Karnul failed, since Captain Popov's battalions had held the Morava division in the Visker Hills, and Alexander's counterattack pushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> back on both flanks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Slivnitsa decided the war. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> were back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> by 24 November, and on 27 November the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a>, with Alexander leading the final attack, defeated them at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pirot">Pirot</a>. Austrian and Russian intervention brought a ceasefire on the 28th. Serbian casualties are given as 6,800 against 2,300 Bulgarian, though Serbian sources give other figures. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a> of 1886 effectively confirmed the unification, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> continued to oppose Alexander, and Russophile officers deposed him in August 1886.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Slivnitsa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Bulgarians won the decisive <b>Battle of Slivnitsa</b> and advanced to take Pirot, and withdrew only when Austria-Hungary declared it would join on Serbia's side; the peace of Bucharest of 19 February 1886 left the border unchanged.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-serbia">Kingdom of Serbia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His reign brought defeat by Bulgaria at <b>Slivnitsa</b> in 1885 and domestic unrest, and he abdicated in 1889 in favour of his son Alexander.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-serbia">Kingdom of Serbia</a></li><li><blockquote>Unification with Eastern Rumelia in 1885 was defended by victory over Serbia at <b>Slivnitsa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-bulgaria">Principality of Bulgaria</a></li></ul></section>
